   "text": "What is the maximum number of points that B could have scored in the tournament?",
   "ans": "10",
   "exp": "Core deduction: C = 8 pts needs 2W 2D 1L (1W+5D is impossible). E = 5 pts needs 1W 2D 2L (0W 5D exceeds the 4 draws). C's only loss is to B, and A is unbeaten, so A–C is a draw. Draws so far: A–F, A–C, plus C's second draw and E's two draws. If C–E were not a draw, there would be at least 5 draws, so C–E is a draw and E has exactly one more draw (E–x). So the 4 draws are A–F, A–C, C–E and E–x. Hence A beat B, D, E: A = 3W 2D = 11. C beat D and F. F has no win and no draw except possibly with E, so B, C and D all beat F. B lost to A; B > 8 and B < 11, so B = 9 (3W) or 10 (3W 1D, the draw being B–E). Checking every combination of the open matches B–D, B–E, D–E, E–F under the distinct-points rule leaves exactly four tables:\n(i) B drew E, beat D; D beat E; E beat F → A11 B10 C8 D6 E5 F1.\n(ii) D beat B, D drew E; B beat E; E beat F → A11 B9 C8 D7 E5 F1.\n(iii) B beat D; E beat B; D beat E; E drew F → A11 B9 C8 D6 E5 F2.\n(iv) D beat B; B beat E; E beat D; E drew F → A11 B9 C8 D6 E5 F2. B cannot reach 12 (that would exceed A's 11 while B is second), and 11 would tie A. Table (i), with B drawing E and beating C, D, F, gives B = 10. Answer: 10.",
