   "type": "tita",
   "text": "For how many positive integers n ≤ 1000 is n³ − n divisible by 48?",
   "ans": "312",
   "exp": "n³ − n = (n − 1)n(n + 1) is always divisible by 3, so we need 16. If n is odd, (n − 1)(n + 1) is a product of consecutive even numbers, divisible by 8 always and by 16 exactly when one of them is a multiple of 8, i.e. n ≡ 1 or 7 (mod 8): 2 × 125 = 250 values. If n is even, n ± 1 are odd, so 16 | n: 62 values. Total 312. Trap: assuming the product of three consecutive integers is always divisible by 48 (answering 1000) or counting only odd n (250).",
